From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Norman Herms <dockseed@proton.me>
Date: Mon, 12 Oct 2026 19:28:36 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] drm/etnaviv: suspend the core before disabling runtime PM on
 remove

When the etnaviv-gpu driver is unbound after the master has been
unbound, as at rmmod, the driver core resumes the device before
remove() and drops its reference with pm_runtime_put_sync() before
calling it. etnaviv uses autosuspend with a 200 ms delay, and the
resume has just updated the last busy time, so the put only arms the
autosuspend timer. etnaviv_gpu_platform_remove() then disables runtime
PM, which cancels the timer, and the driver core marks the device
suspended without calling the suspend callback. The clock enable done by
etnaviv_gpu_rpm_resume() is never undone.

Without this, every such unbind leaves the register, bus and core clocks of
the core enabled for good, because the enable count lives in the clock
framework and survives the driver. Seen on the Radxa Cubie A7S
(Allwinner A733 NPU): after rmmod and modprobe of etnaviv, the NPU
module clock and its bus and memory bus gates stay on while the core is
runtime suspended.

Stop using autosuspend before disabling runtime PM, as
devm_pm_runtime_enable() does on teardown, so the device is suspended
right away through the driver's own callback.

Tested on Radxa Cubie A7S: after each rmmod etnaviv the NPU clocks are
released and their gates are off.

Fixes: a8c21a5451d8 ("drm/etnaviv: add initial etnaviv DRM driver")
Assisted-by: LLM
---
 drivers/gpu/drm/etnaviv/etnaviv_gpu.c | 6 ++++++
 1 file changed, 6 insertions(+)

diff --git a/drivers/gpu/drm/etnaviv/etnaviv_gpu.c b/drivers/gpu/drm/etnaviv/etnaviv_gpu.c
index c4375859dfe9..73ce773e734c 100644
--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/etnaviv/etnaviv_gpu.c
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/etnaviv/etnaviv_gpu.c
@@ -1992,6 +1992,12 @@ static void etnaviv_gpu_platform_remove(struct platform_device *pdev)
 	struct etnaviv_gpu *gpu = dev_get_drvdata(&pdev->dev);
 
 	component_del(&pdev->dev, &gpu_ops);
+	/*
+	 * The driver core resumed the device for the unbind, and with
+	 * autosuspend its put only armed the timer that disabling runtime
+	 * PM cancels. Suspend now, so the clocks are released.
+	 */
+	pm_runtime_dont_use_autosuspend(&pdev->dev);
 	pm_runtime_disable(&pdev->dev);
 
 	mutex_destroy(&gpu->lock);
